{
  "summary": "This experiment tested the effect of multiprofile aggregation (avgpool) by switching from single-profile to multiprofile while holding the scvi_n200 RNA encoder and all other settings constant relative to llm_summary_003, yielding a validation accuracy of 0.650 (F1 0.628) versus 0.520 (F1 0.498) for the single-profile configuration. Because only the profile/aggregation design changed, this clean single-variable comparison indicates that aggregating multiple profiles per cell with a simple average pool captures substantially more discriminative signal than a single profile—a ~13-point gain far exceeding the ~3.5-point encoder improvement observed earlier. It remains uncertain whether a more sophisticated aggregation strategy (e.g., transformer-based) could push accuracy further, whether the multiprofile gain generalizes to other RNA encoders, or whether combining multiprofile aggregation with the image modality or fusion could yield additional improvements."
}

